WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.060
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:08.520
Here you see people that don’t exist.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:09.620
How can that be?

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:15.180
Well, they don’t exist because these images
were created with a neural network-based learning

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:21.000
method by the name StyleGAN2, which can not
only create eye-poppingly detailed looking

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:30.619
images, but it can also fuse these people
together, or generate cars, churches, horses,

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:32.310
and of course, cats.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:38.329
The even cooler thing is that many of these
techniques allow us to exert artistic control

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:40.199
over these images.

00:00:40.199 --> 00:00:42.609
So how does that happen?

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:45.339
How do we control a neural network?

00:00:45.339 --> 00:00:48.819
It happens through exploring latent spaces.

00:00:48.819 --> 00:00:50.769
And what is that?

00:00:50.769 --> 00:00:56.309
A latent space is a made-up place where we
are trying to organize data in a way that

00:00:56.309 --> 00:00:59.519
similar things are close to each other.

00:00:59.519 --> 00:01:04.159
What you see here is a 2D latent space for
generating different fonts.

00:01:04.159 --> 00:01:10.490
It is hard to explain why these fonts are
similar, but most of us would agree that they

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:13.460
indeed share some common properties.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:18.750
The cool thing here is that we can explore
this latent space with our cursor, and generate

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.479
all kinds of new fonts.

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:25.189
You can try this work in your browser, the
link is available in the video description.

00:01:25.189 --> 00:01:32.369
And, luckily, we can build a latent space,
not only for fonts, but, for nearly anything.

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:37.479
I am a light transport researcher by trade,
so in this earlier paper, we were interested

00:01:37.479 --> 00:01:43.329
in generating somewhat hundreds of variants
of a material model to populate this scene.

00:01:43.329 --> 00:01:48.969
In this latent space, we can concoct all of
these really cool digital material models.

00:01:48.969 --> 00:01:53.299
A link to this work is also available in the
video description.

00:01:53.299 --> 00:01:59.280
So let’s recap - one of the cool things
we can do with latent spaces is generate new

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.310
images that are somewhat similar.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:05.210
But there is a problem.

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:11.650
As we go into nearly any direction, not just
one thing, but many things about the image

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:12.690
change.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:18.860
For instance, as we explore the space of fonts
here, not just the width of the font changes,

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.560
everything changes.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:26.060
Or if we explore materials here, not just
the shininess or the colors of the material

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:28.470
change, everything changes.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:32.280
This is great to explore if we can do it in
real time.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.690
If I change this parameter, not just the car
shape changes, the foreground changes, the

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:41.390
background changes…again, everything changes!

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:47.560
So, these are nice and intuitive controls,
but not interpretable controls.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.480
Can we get that somehow?

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:56.120
The answer is, yes, not everything must change,
this previous technique is based on StyleGAN2

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:01.790
and is called StyleFlow, and it can take an
input photo of a test subject, and edit a

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:04.240
number of meaningful parameters.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:10.420
Age, expression, lighting, pose, you name
it.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:15.660
For instance, it could also grew Elon musk
a majestic beard.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:20.490
And that’s not all, because Elon Musk is
not the only person who got a beard.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:25.950
Look, this is me here, after I got locked
up for dropping my papers.

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:29.610
And I spent so long in there, that I grew
a beard.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:33.280
Or I mean, this neural network gave me one.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:38.430
And since the punishment for dropping your
papers is not short…in fact, it is quite

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.580
long…this happened.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:41.650
Ouch.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:46.290
I hereby promise to never drop my papers,
ever again.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:49.730
You will also have to hold on to yours too,
so stay alert.

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:54.290
So, apparently interpretable controls already
exist.

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:57.840
And I wonder, how far can we push this concept?

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.650
Beard or no beard is great, but what about
cars, what about paintings?

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:10.030
Well, this new technique found a way to navigate
these latent spaces and introduces 3 amazing

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:15.770
new examples of interpretable controls that
I haven’t seen anywhere else yet.

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:19.160
One, it can change the car geometry.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:28.250
We can change the sportiness of a car, and
even ask the design to be more or less boxy.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:34.450
Note that there is some additional damage
here, but we can counteract that by changing

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:40.040
the foreground to our taste, for instance,
add some grass in there.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:43.220
Two, it can repaint paintings.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:53.690
We can change the roughness of the brush strokes,
simplify the style 

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:57.650
or even rotate the model.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:03.840
This way, we can create or adjust a painting
without having to even touch a paintbrush.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.660
Three, facial expressions.

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:11.150
First, when I started reading this paper,
I was a little suspicious.

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:18.130
I have seen these controls before so I looked
at it like this, but as I saw how well it

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:21.540
did, I went more… like this.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:28.940
And this paper can do way more, for instance,
it can add lipstick, change the shape of the

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:34.440
mouth or the eyes, and do all this with very
little collateral damage to the remainder

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:35.810
of the image.

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:37.060
Loving it.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:42.830
It can also find and blur the background similarly
to those amazing portrait mode photos that

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:45.120
newer smartphones can do.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.010
And, of course, it can also do the usual suspects.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:54.230
Adjusting the age, hairstyle, or growing a
beard.

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:59.560
So with that, there we go, now, with the power
of neural network-based learning methods,

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:07.020
we can create new car designs, can repaint
paintings without ever touching a paintbrush,

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:09.400
and give someone a shave.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:13.840
It truly feels like we are living in a science
fiction world.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:36.380
What 

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:55.210
a time 

00:06:55.210 --> 00:07:03.699
to be alive!

00:07:03.699 --> 00:07:13.389
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

